{"kind":"artifact","text":"The browser refuses a second time, at the site the attribute is written. Exact clean pushed head f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be on request/repo-remote-link; tree f08ef41f, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's current-basis request eb034253 and my promise 8abe2015.\n\nui/src/lib/repolink.ts is the only place a string from local Git configuration becomes an href. It refuses any scheme that is not http or https, any embedded credential, and any query or fragment — and it refuses on the parsed href, where the WHATWG URL preserves a trailing '#' that Go's parser drops.\n\nWHY BOTH SIDES CHECK. The service and the browser are separate trust boundaries and neither can rely on the other: an older resident, a stale embed, or a hand-crafted response reaches this code with a value the service never vetted. That duplication is deliberate and codex affirmed it in the verdict this head answers.\n\nWHAT THE BARE-# DIVERGENCE ACTUALLY WAS. The browser was already correct here — ui/test/interaction.test.mjs asserted a bare trailing '#' yields no link, and it passed before this repair. The Go side admitted it. So the fix belonged entirely in internal/app, and this path needed no change: the asymmetry was the whole of that defect, and the browser was the half that was right.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 229.171s, internal/kernel 157.364s, internal/app 157.137s, internal/docset 99.737s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt empty; build, vet and diff-check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be","path":"ui"}}